 ### 6.4 Chris blind scores (continuous — the feedback loop)
 
 See §9. Each submission creates one `chris_score` chunk per criterion where a note exists, plus one `exemplar` chunk containing: session extraction (§8.1) + Chris's six scores + his note. Exemplars are what make the scorer sound like Chris.
 
+Implementation notes (session 6): the scorecard is read from the Sheet by `mentorScores()` (`/lib/mentorScores.js` — the same reader the screen uses; structured blind items and hand-posted lines both count), never taken from the request. **Exemplar text** is organised by form line — what Mark said (rendered from the extraction's own fields: connection counts and each chain flagged complete / partial-with-what's-missing / prompted, the two comparisons, the prescription chain, equipment flags), then the mentor's number — capped at 1.8K tokens so three fit the slot; it carries **no AI score, rationale, or rewrite**. It is **embedded on `extractionToText(extraction)`**, the same function that builds the query, not on the rendered text (`embed_text`, `store.insertChunks`) — like is compared with like, and his numbers don't compress exemplars into one similarity band (the session 1 finding). The blind form has one note field, so `chris_score` note chunks are the note split into single points (same splitter as comments), each tagged to the lines it speaks to and stating what he gave them; they ride in the Chris slot. Metadata holds the full extraction, `blind`, and **`ai_at_submit` — pinned at first ingest and never moved by a rescore or re-ingest**. Idempotent; a changed scorecard or a re-extraction (`--reextract`) replaces the exemplar by supersession; exactly one live exemplar per mentor per session. Chris's exemplar supersedes the session's `past_session` chunk (§6.7); Gates's and Mike's are stored (inter-rater data) but supersede nothing and are not retrieved as exemplars.
